     1	# Relay supply-chain boundary
     2	
     3	The authoritative machine-readable contract is
     4	`relay/supply-chain-source-v1.json`. Its generated provenance and inventory
     5	share a deterministic linkage digest with the exact four-entry relay asset
     6	manifest. The linkage covers the manifest schema, source revision and source
     7	aggregate, build recipe revision and aggregate, toolchain manifest, accepted
     8	archive, and ordered asset size/hash tuples without creating a circular file
     9	hash dependency.
    10	
    11	Each fixed component record is checked against authoritative evidence before
    12	generation: relay source and recipe records resolve to pinned Git commits and
    13	file aggregates; the compiler/linker and standard library resolve to the
    14	selected host archive in the pinned toolchain manifest; and each component ID
    15	has one approved SPDX, license-text hash, notice obligation, and distribution
    16	classification. Source URLs must exactly match the component's commit or
    17	version-specific HTTPS allowlist; branches, tags, `latest`, placeholders,
    18	queries, fragments, alternate hosts, and alternate archive names fail closed.
    19	
    20	Run the complete read-only audit from a clean checkout:
    21	
    22	```sh
    23	make relay-supply-chain-audit
    24	```
    25	
    26	Regenerate after an approved metadata/input change, then run the focused tests:
    27	
    28	```sh
    29	make relay-supply-chain-generate
    30	make relay-supply-chain-test relay-asset-manifest-test
    31	```
    32	
    33	For the complete operator sequence, use the
    34	[relay asset release, update, audit, and rollback runbook](relay-asset-release-runbook.md).
    35	It preserves the accepted split source/recipe pins, exact bytes and manifest,
    36	and the boundary between M2 unsigned bundle inputs and later M5 release work.
    37	
    38	## M2 and M5 ownership
    39	
    40	| Control | Milestone | Concrete owner / scope | Evidence / gate |
    41	| --- | --- | --- | --- |
    42	| Source pinning | M2 | Relay build — `TASK-260715-27uz4n` | Full Git revision, repository/relay trees, and SHA-256 for every compiled repository file |
    43	| Build reproducibility | M2 | Relay build — `TASK-260715-27uz4n` | Pinned build-recipe revision, Go compiler/linker archive, no-container base-image declaration, target matrix, environment, and command |
    44	| Asset integrity | M2 | Relay asset packaging — `TASK-260715-1ue4oy` | Accepted archive SHA-256, four ordered executable sizes/hashes, identity, and shared manifest linkage digest |
    45	| License notices | M2 | Relay supply-chain audit — `TASK-260715-vtot05` | Approved SPDX/license hashes and deterministically generated `relay/PRODUCT_NOTICES.txt` |
    46	| Application signing | M5 | Apple release pipeline — `TASK-260715-3sk5cd` | Signed application/archive verification; no signing occurs in the M2 relay build |
    47	| Notarization | M5 | Apple release pipeline — `TASK-260715-387eof` | Submission, acceptance, and staple verification |
    48	| Release attestation | M5 | Release engineering — `TASK-260715-1gzhnk` | Release-level attestation bound to signed deliverables, not this unsigned build provenance |
    49	| Distribution approval | M5 | Release approver — `TASK-260715-312u2k` | Explicit approval for the selected release channel |
    50	
    51	M2 produces unsigned, hash-verifiable bundled inputs and never publishes or
    52	installs them independently. M5 consumes those exact inputs and owns all Apple
    53	identity, notarization, release attestation, and distribution decisions.
    54	
    55	## Runtime policy
    56	
    57	The application may execute only the relay asset selected from its bundled,
    58	generated catalog after the existing size, SHA-256, platform, and identity
    59	checks. Application and relay runtime source may not contain HTTP download
    60	clients or executable-code fetch paths. Tool/archive acquisition remains an
    61	explicit build-host provisioning step using immutable versioned URLs and
    62	SHA-256 verification; build execution itself is offline.
    63	
    64	The audit uses an immutable, non-empty static scan contract over `App/`,
    65	`Sources/`, and `relay/` for Swift, Go, Objective-C/C/C++, and header source,
    66	including `.cxx`, `.hh`, `.hpp`, and `.hxx`. Every regular file in those roots
    67	must classify as a scanned source kind, a structurally named Go `_test.go`
    68	file, or one exact reviewed non-runtime path recorded in `runtimePolicy`.
    69	Unknown extensions, new unclassified files, symlinks, missing roots,
    70	empty/partial policy, and a scan that finds no source files fail closed.
    71	
    72	The forbidden surface includes Foundation URL/network loaders (including
    73	ambiguous `Data(contentsOf:)` and `String(contentsOf:)`), Objective-C `NSData`
    74	URL selectors, libcurl, C-family `system`/`popen`/spawn/exec entry points,
    75	Swift processes and shell/download commands, Go network/process/plugin imports
    76	and calls regardless of import alias or grouping, and HTTP locators. Explicit
    77	`Data` and `String` reads from `URL(fileURLWithPath:)` remain permitted.
    78	Checks run over comment-free per-language lexical tokens. C-family input also
    79	uses the language's non-nesting block comments, normalizes escaped newlines and
    80	both standard and alternative token-paste spellings, and rejects forbidden
    81	symbol references, aliases, address-taking, and token-pasted fragments. Swift
    82	keeps nested-comment semantics, tokenizes escaped identifiers, inspects normal
    83	and pound-delimited raw-string interpolation, and rejects process type or
    84	ambiguous `contentsOf` constructor references. Objective-C rejects dynamic
    85	selector/reflection paths. Go uses non-nesting block comments, and import
    86	parsing is independent of aliases, grouping, raw strings, and
    87	comment/whitespace placement.
    88	`scripts/` is outside the immutable runtime roots because it is build/test
    89	tooling.
    90	
    91	This gate verifies the complete classified repository runtime surface against
    92	the enumerated executable-fetch/download mechanisms. It is not a general
    93	semantic proof for arbitrary future languages: any new or unclassified file
    94	kind fails until the policy and scanner are reviewed together.
